But the problem is that the "deliberate overkill" serves to
obscure the code.  It's a balance; I'm certainly against
gratuitous use of the ?: operator, or overly long functions,
or overly clever/cutesy compaction of code.

But adding unnecessary verbiage detracts from the clarity of
the code. And in my experience, using personal idioms instead of the
commonly used (and taught) language idioms is more likely to
confuse those wet behind the ears new hires than help them--god
knows I've managed enough of those.

> Saying that the construct has already reached the
> stage the compiler needs isn't implying that it has
> yet reached a form sufficient to educate the middle
> four sigmas of the coding population.

Right, but the reason I write it the way I do is NOT for the
compiler.  It's for the human reader.
> Respected (worshipped by quite a few
> of us) computer scientist Donald Knuth, author of
> the textbook series "The Art of Computer
> Programming", inventor of TeX and METAFONT and
> grandfather of LaTeX, and far more accomplishements
> than I know, much less can list, recommends a whole
> other level of the same medicine, which he calls
> "Literate Programming", in which the entire text
> genesis of the software is an intimate part of the
> source code.

I've used literate programming in CWeb on a couple projects.
I daresay that Knuth would HATE useless verbiage that 
detracts from the readability of the code.  You can see 
an example of his code (CWeb code, Literate programming 
done in C) at:
 http://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/programs/rost.w

It's far terser than I would advocate.

Remember that Knuth is even an advocate of using "goto"
in certain cases (he wrote "GOTO considered harmful considered
harmful", an indictment of Dijkstra's popular "GOTO
considered harmful" paper).  He believes in using having the
documentation written with the code, but he believes in having
both be minimalistic.  Read the TeX source some time for an
eye-opener.

> comp.risks

I'm sure all of us who write code have followed comp.risks
at one point or another in our programming careers.  If 
not, it's certainly worth a look.

(Also FYI, even though NULL may not be implemented as "all bits zero"
on every platform, a NULL pointer is guaranteed to test false on
every platform.  And casting of NULL is a horrible idea--it can
never cause correct behavior, but CAN suppress warnings if someone
has defined NULL to something stupid--which I've seen happen before
with people who didn't understand that pointers and longs can be
different sizes on some machines.

